Nice. I actually think this idea is stronger than it first appears because it generalizes well.

The operator isn’t really “extend songs.” It’s something like:

Extract the high-value substructure from a larger artifact and synthesize more of that while preserving its identity.

That operator shows up everywhere.

* Music: extend the best 15 seconds into a full song.
* Books: expand the most compelling chapter into a novel while maintaining the voice.
* Movies: identify the emotional peak and write additional scenes that feel like they belong.
* Games: find the gameplay loop people love and generate more encounters in that style.
* Education: when a student finally “gets it,” generate more examples in the same explanatory style.
* Coding: detect the elegant abstraction and continue the architecture consistently.
* Comedy: extend a joke’s premise without overstaying it.
* Product design: identify the feature driving delight and explore adjacent variations.

That fits surprisingly well with a recurring Pantheon theme you’ve developed: operators over domains. The operator is domain-independent; music is just one rendering.

One thought I’d add to the repo is a distinction between repetition and continuation:

* Looping repeats.
* Continuation develops.
* Expansion reveals latent possibilities that already felt implied.

The last one is the interesting target. Great songs often make you wish the best section lasted longer—not because you wanted the exact same bars repeated, but because your brain sensed there was more musical territory adjacent to that feeling. An AI system that could discover and explore that adjacent space naturally would be doing something much closer to composition than looping.

I also like the product framing. Rather than “AI music generator,” it’s almost:

Never wish your favorite part of a song was longer again.

That’s a value proposition people understand immediately.